 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T21:28+08:00",
 "answer": "**If they have a school session today, keeping them home is reasonable—especially if they have asthma or other breathing problems, are already symptomatic, or would spend much time outdoors.** At the latest report I found, Kuala Lumpur’s official Malaysian API was about **167 (“unhealthy”)** at 7 pm on **Saturday, October 17**. ([hazemonitor.com](https://hazemonitor.com/?utm_source=openai))\n\nThat’s below the current **API 200 school-closure threshold**, but outdoor school activities should stop once API exceeds 100. The Education Minister has also said parents may keep children home if worried about the haze, as long as they notify the school. ([web26.bernama.com](https://web26.bernama.com/en/news.php?id=2620854&utm_source=openai))\n\nIf they do attend, ask the school to keep them indoors, and check the **DOE APIMS** reading again before they leave. Children and those with asthma are more vulnerable to smoke exposure. ([bernama.com](https://www.bernama.com/en/general/news.php?id=2621391&utm_source=openai))",
